# Design mockups are a specification, not an inspiration

<!-- core -->
Build it exactly as drawn: layout, spacing, sizes, colours, typography, radii, shadows, icons, and
the literal copy — typos included. **No unrequested improvements** — if the mockup looks wrong, say
so and build it as drawn unless the user agrees to change it. Reuse existing tokens where they
already match; add new ones where the mockup demands values the system lacks, rather than bending
the mockup to fit.

**Ask rather than invent.** A mockup shows the resting state; every other state exists only if
named — empty input, zero results, hover/focus/disabled, loading, error, validation, breakpoints,
overflow. A picker must not offer what submit would reject; whether it filters is a spec question,
not a style one. A scrollable value picker has both directions stated. A control its sibling
already has elsewhere reuses that composable, not a rebuild. Batch the questions up front; ask
mid-build the moment a real blocker appears. Verify by running the app and doing the thing —
interact, screenshot, compare side by side; a green suite or a read diff misses a fill colour, a
missing border, a picker that offers what submit rejects.

<!-- /core -->

Applies to any mockup the user presents — an image, a Figma frame or link, a screenshot, an HTML
prototype, a PDF.

## Match it

Layout, spacing, sizes, colours, typography (family, weight, size, line height, letter spacing),
border radii, shadows, icons, and the literal copy shown. Reproduce the mockup's text verbatim,
typos included, unless told otherwise.

## No unrequested improvements

Do not "fix" alignment, rebalance whitespace, substitute a different component, modernise the
styling, or swap the palette because something looks off.

The instinct to improve is the failure mode here. A mockup that looks slightly wrong is usually
either deliberate or already agreed with someone you are not talking to.

## Ask rather than invent

Mockups are static and always underspecify. The moment something the mockup does not answer comes
up, ask — do not guess, and do not silently pick a default. "Match the mockup" covers the resting
state only; name every other state. Typical gaps:

- Behaviour of interactive elements.
- Hover, focus, active, selected and disabled states.
- Empty input, zero results, loading, error and overflow states.
- Validation rules and messages.
- Whether a picker filters its options or only validates on submit. One that offers a selection
  submit will reject is a spec gap, not a style gap — the picker should never offer it.
- Both directions of a scrollable or wheel value picker; "scrollable" alone reads as one.
- Responsive behaviour at breakpoints the mockup does not show.
- What happens with long text or large numbers.
- Exact assets or icon sets.
- Which real data feeds which element.

Where a gap is small and clearly answered by an established convention already in
the codebase, follow that convention and say so.

## Reuse the sibling's component

A control that already exists elsewhere in the app — a workout row, a button variant — is the same
composable, not a rebuild. Find the sibling before writing one, and check the diff for a second
copy. Rebuilt siblings are where style drift lives: a row rendered two ways, a button missing the
border its sibling has.

## Verify by running it before claiming it is done

Run the app, open the screen and do the thing — type, select, submit, scroll the picker both ways —
then screenshot and compare against the mockup side by side. Reading the diff or a green test suite
is not verification: a selected-state fill, a filter-vs-error gap, a missing border only show on
the screen.

Applies globally, all projects, no exceptions.
